.TH CLAUDE-USER 1 "2026-10-29" "agent-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claude-user \- run Claude Code as the account a directory belongs to
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude-user
[\fBshow\fR [\fIDIR\fR]]
.br
.B claude-user run
[\fB\-a\fR \fIaccount\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] [\fIclaude-args\fR ...]
.br
.B claude-user list
.br
.B claude-user add
.I account
[\fIdir\fR ...]
.br
.B claude-user rm
[\fIdir\fR ...]
.br
.B claude-user default
[\fIaccount\fR]
.br
.B claude-user init
.I account
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claude-user
keeps several Claude Code logins, such as a work and a personal account, apart
on one machine. Each account has its own config dir, which Claude Code is
pointed at with
.BR CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR :
credentials, settings, sessions, prompt history and memory all live there.
The default account uses
.I ~/.claude
and
.IR ~/.claude.json ,
as Claude Code does with no
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
set; any other account
.I name
uses
.IR ~/.claude\-name .
.PP
Which account a directory belongs to is set per machine in the config file:
a directory belongs to the account of the longest configured directory that
contains it, matched on whole path components, and to the default account if
none does. Directories are resolved through symlinks before matching.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR show " [\fIDIR\fR]"
Print the account
.I DIR
(default: the current directory) belongs to, its config dir, and the
configured directory that decided it. This is the default command.
.TP
.B run
Run
.B claude
with the remaining arguments as the account of the current directory, or as
.I account
with
.BR \-a .
For the default account
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
is removed from the environment; for any other it is set to the account's
config dir, which must exist. A
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
already set is kept unless
.B \-a
is given. Its own options must come first; everything after them, or after
.BR \-\- ,
goes to
.BR claude .
To use it for every session:
.B alias claude='claude-user run'
.TP
.B list
Print the default account and each configured directory with its account.
.TP
.BI add " account" " \fR[\fPdir\fR ...]"
Make each
.I dir
(default: the current directory) and everything in it belong to
.IR account ,
replacing any entry for it. Each
.I dir
must be below
.IR ~ :
set the default account for
.I ~
itself.
.TP
.BR rm " [\fIdir\fR ...]"
Remove the entry for each
.I dir
(default: the current directory). Nothing is removed if one has no entry.
.TP
.BR default " [\fIaccount\fR]"
Print, or set, the account that owns
.I ~/.claude
on this machine. Which one does can differ between machines.
.TP
.BI init " account"
Create
.I ~/.claude\-account
and copy
.IR CLAUDE.md ,
.IR settings.json ,
.I keybindings.json
and
.I skills/
into it from
.IR ~/.claude .
Credentials, sessions and plugins are not copied: log in with
.B claude-user run \-a
.I account
and
.BR /login .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/claude\-user/config
Lines of
.BI default " = account"
followed by
.BI dir " = account"
lines under a
.B [dirs]
header;
.B #
starts a comment. For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
default = work

[dirs]
~/src = personal
~/src/impulse = work
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_USER_CONFIG
Use this config file instead.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the config file lives, if set.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR claude\-sync (1)
